// Per-combination local paragraph and second FAQ, keyed "service|area".
// Added Sept 2026 so each service x area page carries content that is
// specific to that pairing rather than repeated from its parent pages.
// Same rules as combo-detail.ts: no prices, response times, guarantees or
// claims about past jobs; describes what the building stock implies.

export type ComboExtra = { local: string; faq2: { q: string; a: string } };

export const comboExtras: Record<string, ComboExtra> = {
  "emergency-plumbing|bromley": {
    "local": "In a Bromley semi or detached house, the first job in an emergency is usually straightforward: stop the water, then work out where it is going. The indoor stopcock is often in the kitchen or a utility room, but on older plots it can sit in the garage or near the front boundary under a small lid. If the house has a loft tank, shutting the mains will not stop what is already stored above you, so the tank valve matters too. Supplies that run out to a garage, workshop or garden room tend to have their own valve where they leave the house, and that is worth labelling. At night, what slows an engineer down here is rarely traffic. It is finding the right house on a dark road, set back behind hedges, and getting through a locked side gate. Leave a porch light on, open the gate if you can, and say whether the van can pull onto the drive.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The pipe to our garden office has burst. Do we have to turn off the whole house?",
      "a": "Not always. A supply that runs out to a garden room, garage or workshop often has its own isolating valve, either where the pipe leaves the house or under a small cover near the outbuilding. Turn that off and the house keeps its water. If you cannot find one, use the main stopcock and leave it off until the engineer is with you. Tell us on the phone how far the outbuilding is from the house, so they bring enough pipe."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|croydon": {
    "local": "Croydon emergencies split three ways, and the first step differs in each. In a 1930s semi, you will normally find the stopcock beneath the stairs or the kitchen sink, and turning it off is something you can do yourself straight away. In a flat above a shop, it is harder. The supply for the flat sometimes passes through the shop unit, and at night that means a closed shutter and a keyholder who has to be found. In the town centre towers, the valve that matters is usually on a riser in a locked cupboard, so the building’s out-of-hours contact is the next call after ours. For engineers, access decides a lot here: entrances at the back of a parade, entry phones that do not work, lifts out of service. If you live above a shop, tell us where your own front door actually is, because it is often round the back rather than on the main road.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The shop below my flat has water coming through its ceiling, and it is closed. Can you still deal with it?",
      "a": "We can deal with anything inside your flat, and that is often where the fault is, because a pipe under your floor shows in the shop ceiling before it shows in your rooms. Turn your stopcock off and ask anyone who can see into the shop whether the dripping slows. If it does, the source is yours. If not, we will need the shop’s keyholder, which usually means the landlord or the out-of-hours number displayed on the shopfront."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|wimbledon": {
    "local": "In one of the bigger Wimbledon houses, a burst on an upper floor can reach the ground floor through two ceilings before anyone downstairs notices. Once the mains and the loft tank are both off, switch off the immersion heater too, because heating an emptied cylinder can burn out the element. If the hot side is leaking, the airing cupboard is where the cylinder and its valves usually live. Nearer the station, the picture changes. Victorian terraces and flats there behave more like inner London, with shared pipework and sometimes a neighbour’s stopcock involved. For night visits to the larger houses, electric gates and alarm systems are the usual hold-ups. Tell us the gate code or leave it open, and let us know if the alarm covers the loft or the rooms we need to reach. On most roads here parking is not the problem; spotting a house number in the dark can be.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Should I turn the boiler off as well as the water?",
      "a": "Yes, if the leak is anywhere on the heating or hot water side. On an older vented system the boiler can keep firing while the small tank that tops up the heating is cut off at the mains, and running it short of water risks damage. Turn it off at the switch or the programmer and leave it off. The engineer will refill and check the system before it runs again, rather than you guessing at the pressure."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|islington": {
    "local": "In an Islington terrace, the stopcock can be in the basement, in a vault under the front pavement, or behind a panel someone boxed in years ago. If the house is split into flats, one supply may still serve every floor, so turning it off affects the neighbours and they need to know. While you wait, move rugs, books and furniture out from under any wet ceiling. Old lime plaster can hold a lot of water before it gives, and it is far easier to save the contents than the ceiling. A bucket under the drip and a towel to stop it spreading along the boards help too. For a night visit, small things slow us down: a basement flat reached down steps behind a locked railing gate, a house number that is hard to read on a long terrace, and permit parking that means the van may be a short walk away. Tell us which door is yours.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Water is coming through an old decorative ceiling. Should I make a hole to let it out?",
      "a": "Not on a decorative or listed ceiling. Piercing a bulge is sometimes done on modern plasterboard, but on lime plaster with cornicing it can bring a whole section down, and putting that back is specialist work. Turn the water off, keep people out of the room, put containers under the drips and leave the ceiling to the engineer. Photograph it now as well, both for your insurer and for anyone who has to restore it later."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|clapham": {
    "local": "In a Clapham house share, the first part of any emergency goes on working out who is in charge. Pick one person to call, one to find the stopcock and one to meet the engineer at the door. If a washing machine or dishwasher hose has failed, there is often a small lever valve on the pipe behind the machine, and turning that off shuts down the appliance while the taps and bathrooms carry on working. In the Victorian terraces the main stopcock tends to be under the stairs, in a cellar, or under a hatch in the front hall floor. At night, access is simple in principle and messy in practice. The doorbell might not be heard from a top-floor room, and the person who rang us might have gone out. Give us the mobile number of whoever will actually open the door. If the landlord or agent needs to authorise work, having their out-of-hours number ready saves time.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our washing machine flooded the kitchen but the water has stopped. Is it still an emergency?",
      "a": "Probably not, once the supply is off and nothing is still running. Leave the machine unplugged with its valve closed, mop up, and check the ceiling below if another flat sits under your kitchen. It becomes urgent again if water keeps appearing, the floor stays wet underneath, or the downstairs neighbour reports a drip. Otherwise it can be booked as an ordinary repair in daylight, which is cheaper for you than a night visit."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|hackney": {
    "local": "Hackney emergencies often come down to who holds which key. In a warehouse conversion, concrete floors do not soak water up the way timber does, so it spreads sideways across a unit and towards the next one before it finds a way down. Exposed pipework helps, because the fault is usually visible, but the valve for your unit may be in a service cupboard on the landing. On the estates, it matters whether you rent or own. Council and housing association tenants should ring their landlord’s out-of-hours repairs line as well, while leaseholders usually own the pipes inside their flat and can call us direct. Night access can be the slow part. Fob-only doors, deck access blocks and gated courtyards all need someone to come down or pass on a code. Tell us exactly which entrance serves your stairwell, because large blocks here often have several.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I am a leaseholder in an ex-council block. Who pays for the repair?",
      "a": "It depends where the leak is. Pipes that serve only your flat are normally your responsibility, while the risers and mains that serve the whole block usually fall to the freeholder. Your lease sets out the detail. In an emergency, stop the damage first and settle who pays afterwards. We will write down where the failure was on the invoice, so the conversation with the housing office is based on what the engineer actually found."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|fulham": {
    "local": "Being based on Fulham High Street means we know the usual layout of a converted SW6 terrace well, and that shapes the first advice we give. If dirty water is coming up into a lower-ground bath or shower, the fix starts upstairs: every flat on that stack needs to stop flushing and running water until it is cleared, so knock on doors or message the neighbours straight away. If clean water is dripping through a ceiling, it is the upstairs neighbour’s stopcock that matters, not yours. For a basement taking water from outside or below, switch off power to that floor if water is near sockets. At night, the practical details are front area steps behind a gate, a shared front door with several bells, and permit parking on most residential streets. Tell us which bell is yours, and put a light on at the lower-ground entrance if that is the door we need.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Sewage is coming up in my lower-ground shower. Is it my drain or the building’s?",
      "a": "If you are the lowest flat on a shared stack, usually the building’s. A blockage further down makes waste from the flats above back up at the lowest outlet, which happens to be yours. Stop using your own water too, and ask the flats above to hold off. We clear the blockage first. Afterwards we can look at whether an anti-flood valve on your outlets would stop it happening again."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|wandsworth": {
    "local": "When a Wandsworth kitchen has been pushed out into the side return, the stopcock often moved with it, or got boxed in behind new units. If you cannot find it, check behind the kick boards under the sink run, in a utility cupboard, and under the stairs where the old kitchen used to meet the hall. An island sink or wet underfloor heating means pipes buried in the new slab, so a leak there shows as a damp or warm patch on the floor rather than a drip. If it is heating water, switch the boiler off too, not just the mains. The riverside blocks are a different job. There, your own flat usually has a valve in a hall or utility cupboard, but the concierge or estate office may need to let us into the building. At night, say whether we are ringing a flat bell or going through a front desk.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Water pressure has dropped across my whole flat in a riverside block. Is it a leak?",
      "a": "Not necessarily. Ask the concierge or a neighbour first, because a fault with the building’s booster pumps or a planned shutdown affects every flat at once, and that is for building management to sort. If only your flat is affected, check the valve in your cupboard is fully open, then look under sinks, around the water heater and at the ceiling below. Water escaping somewhere it should not is the thing to rule out."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|ealing": {
    "local": "Ealing splits neatly for emergency work. In the large Edwardian and interwar houses, the job is often finding which of several bathrooms is actually leaking. Look at the ceiling stain and work out what sits directly above it, then check the floor around the shower tray, bath panel and toilet in that room. If you can isolate just that bathroom, the rest of the house carries on. In the 1930s purpose-built blocks, cold water often comes from a communal tank on the roof rather than straight off the mains, so the valve for your flat may be on a landing or in a cupboard shared with a neighbour. The porter or managing agent usually knows where. At night, blocks with a porter’s lodge may have nobody on duty, so have the agent’s emergency number ready. The larger houses are easier: just tell us whether to use the front door or a side entrance.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I live in a purpose-built block. Can you work on the communal pipework?",
      "a": "We can, but only with the managing agent’s agreement, because communal tanks, risers and pipes serving several flats belong to the building rather than to you. In an emergency we isolate what we safely can to stop the damage, then report back to the agent. If the leak is on pipework serving only your flat, you can instruct us directly. Have the agent’s out-of-hours number ready when you call."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|brixton": {
    "local": "A Brixton emergency often involves a business and a home in the same building: a restaurant at street level and flats above, sharing drains and sometimes a supply. If you run the kitchen, the first step is to stop discharging water. Shut down the dishwasher, stop the sinks and hold off mopping into a floor gully that is already backing up. Find the kitchen’s own isolation valve, which is often separate from the flats’ supply. If you live above and water or waste is appearing, the cause may be in the unit below, so the business’s keyholder becomes the person to reach. For night attendance, access is the thing to plan. Many units have roller shutters, an alarm and a back door onto a service yard. Tell us which entrance to use and whether someone with the alarm code will be there. Stopping on the main roads is often restricted, so a nearby side street helps.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I live above a takeaway and my sink is gurgling and smells of drains. Is that urgent?",
      "a": "It can be. Gurgling and a sewage smell in an upstairs flat often mean a shared drain is partly blocked below you, and grease from a commercial kitchen is a common cause. If water is backing up into your sink or bath, stop using it and call. If it is only the smell, it can usually be booked for daytime. Either way, tell your landlord, because the fix may involve the business’s drainage."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|harrow": {
    "local": "In a Harrow semi, the stopcock tends to be in the hall by the front door, beneath the stairs or in the sink cupboard, but after two or three heating upgrades it is not unusual for it to be old, stiff and never tested. Turn it gently. If it will not move, do not force it with a wrench, because an old stopcock can snap and make things worse. Use the outside stop tap instead, usually in the front garden or pavement under a small lid. If the leak is from a loft conversion bathroom, switch off the lighting circuit for the rooms below, as the water often finds the light fittings first. With a cylinder in the airing cupboard, turn the immersion heater off if hot water is escaping. Night access here is usually simple: a driveway to park on and a front door facing the road. An outside light still helps on streets of near-identical houses.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Water is leaking where new plastic pipe meets the old metal. Can I tighten it?",
      "a": "Turn the water off first rather than trying anything with it live. If it is a compression fitting, a gentle quarter turn sometimes stops a weep, but on old galvanised or iron pipe the thread behind the joint is often corroded and more force can crack it. Wrap it in a towel, put a bowl under it and leave it for the engineer. If it is only weeping, it may not need a night visit."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|balham": {
    "local": "Balham conversions often have a quirk that catches people out at night: the stopcock for the upstairs flat may be in the downstairs flat, or in a shared hallway, because the supply was split after the house was built. If you are upstairs and cannot find yours, ask below, and the other way round. If you are the upper flat and your neighbour says water is coming through, check the obvious sources in your own bathroom first: the seal around the bath, the base of the toilet, the shower tray and the pipes under the basin. Stop using that room while you look. If you are the lower flat and nobody answers upstairs, a letting agent or freeholder may hold a key. For us, access is usually through one of two front doors that sit side by side. Tell us which one, and whether anyone has a spare key to the other flat.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "My downstairs neighbour says I am leaking into their flat, but I cannot see anything. What now?",
      "a": "Stop using your bathroom and kitchen completely for a while and ask them whether the drip slows. If it does, the source is almost certainly on your side, often a failed seal round the bath or shower that only leaks when it is in use. If it carries on regardless, a supply pipe under your floor is more likely, since that leaks whether you use water or not. Either way, the engineer traces it before lifting boards."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|kensington": {
    "local": "In a Kensington mansion block, the flat below is often the first to know about a leak, and the porter is the second. If you are the source, turn off your own valve if you can find it, then go down and warn the flat underneath, because water in these buildings can pass through several storeys of lath and plaster. If water is running near the lift, tell the porter so it can be taken out of use. Where there is no night porter, the managing agent’s emergency number is the way to reach the riser. In stucco houses split into flats, the supply for the whole building may come in at lower-ground level, so that occupant may need to let us in. For night visits, expect an entryphone, a communal door and residents’ parking. Give us the porter’s number or the name on your buzzer, and warn us if the building has a key-only service entrance.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I cannot reach my stopcock because it is behind fitted cupboards. What should I do?",
      "a": "Do not start cutting into fitted joinery in a hurry. First look for a valve on the pipe feeding whatever is leaking, such as under the basin or behind the toilet, and use that. If there is none, the building’s main valve or the outside stop tap will shut off the supply, though in a block it will affect other flats too. Once the emergency is over, having the valve moved somewhere reachable is a sensible small job."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|lewisham": {
    "local": "In a Lewisham terrace, the bathroom usually sits in the back addition above the kitchen, while the stopcock is at the front of the house, under the stairs or in the hall floor. So when the bathroom leaks, water comes through the kitchen ceiling, sometimes into the light fittings, and the valve is at the opposite end of the house. Turn the kitchen lights off at the consumer unit before you go looking. In the towers and estate blocks, the working assumption is different: the leak has probably started above you, and not always directly above. Go up floor by floor and knock, because the flat two storeys up may not know. In the newer town centre blocks, the concierge or site team is the quickest way to find the source. At night, give us the entrance that serves your stairwell, not just the building name, and say whether the lifts are running.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The building manager turned the riser off and the leak stopped, but now nobody has water. What happens next?",
      "a": "That is normal. Isolating a riser cuts every flat it feeds, so the aim is to find the flat with the fault, isolate just that one and get the riser back on as soon as possible. We need access to the source flat to do that, which is why the building manager’s help matters. Once everyone else has water again, the repair in the source flat can be finished properly."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|battersea": {
    "local": "In a Nine Elms or riverside flat, the first thing to find is the utility cupboard. It usually holds the heat interface unit, the cold water valve for the flat and often the washing machine connections, so most leaks can be stopped there. Turn the cold valve off, and if the leak is coming from the heat unit itself, close the valves on the pipes into it if you can see them. Then tell the concierge, because water in a concrete-framed tower can reach the flats below quickly and the building may want to check them. For night visits, these developments usually need the engineer registered with the front desk, and underground car parks often have height limits a van cannot clear, so tell us where a trade vehicle can stop. The terraced streets off the park are the other half of Battersea: converted flats, shared stacks, and a stopcock that is often in the hall of the ground-floor flat.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Does the concierge in my building need to know before you come?",
      "a": "Almost always, in the newer developments. Most front desks keep a list of expected contractors, and some ask for proof of insurance or a works permit before anyone can go up, even at night. Tell them you have called an emergency plumber and give them our number. If the building needs paperwork, we can send it. For a flat in a terrace off the park, none of that applies; we just need to know which door."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|putney": {
    "local": "Putney’s emergencies depend on which end of it you live in. In the mansion blocks near the bridge, a toilet or bath backing up usually means the shared stack is blocked lower down, so the fix is to stop everyone on that stack using water, not just you. Many of these blocks have a porter in the daytime only, so at night the managing agent’s emergency line is how you reach the shared valves and the basement. Out at Roehampton, the blocks are larger and taller. If you rent from the council, report it on their out-of-hours line too, since communal pipework is theirs to repair. For a night visit, give us the block name and the entrance, since several blocks can look alike from the road, and say whether the lift serves your floor. In the Victorian terraces in between, it is the familiar picture: converted flats, and a leak that shows first in the lower one.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Water is coming through the stairwell ceiling, not into my flat. Should I call anyone?",
      "a": "Yes. A leak in a communal stairwell usually comes from a riser or a flat above, and it can get into lighting and lift equipment. Report it to the managing agent or the council’s out-of-hours line straight away, and keep people away from any wet light fittings. We can attend on the building’s instruction, but we will need someone who can open the service cupboards. Photos showing where it is coming from help."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|dulwich": {
    "local": "Most Dulwich houses are single homes on long plots, which makes night emergencies more about distance than about neighbours. A burst in a large period house can run unnoticed on an upper floor, so once the stopcock is off, walk every floor and switch off lighting circuits under any wet ceilings. If it is drainage rather than supply, look inside the inspection chambers if you can lift the covers safely. A full chamber near the house with an empty one further down tells us the blockage sits between the two. Do not lean over or climb into an open chamber in the dark, and put the cover back or mark it clearly. For the engineer, a long garden at night is the obstacle: a side passage with a locked gate, no outside lighting and chambers at the far end. Unlock the gate, switch on any garden lights and tell us roughly where the covers are.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our house is on the Dulwich Estate. Do we need permission for an emergency repair?",
      "a": "Generally not to make it safe. Stopping escaping water or clearing a blocked drain is a repair rather than an alteration. It is the permanent fix that can need thought: if it changes the outside of the house, such as a new external pipe or a different drain position, the scheme of management may ask for consent. The engineer will stop the problem now and point out anything worth checking with the Estate before it is finished."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|greenwich": {
    "local": "In Greenwich, the first question on the phone decides everything else: is water escaping, or has the heating or hot water stopped? In a Georgian or early Victorian house near the town centre, escaping water means finding a stopcock that may be in a basement kitchen or a cellar. Old lead pipe can split along its length rather than at a joint, so look along exposed runs as well as at the fittings. On the Peninsula, a no-heat call in a new tower is often a network issue, while a leak inside the flat is usually stopped at the valves in the utility cupboard. At night, the older streets near the centre are narrow and parking is tight, so the van may have to stop a short way off. The Peninsula blocks tend to have a staffed desk or an out-of-hours number; have it ready so the engineer is not left waiting in the lobby.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Water is leaking from the cupboard with the heat unit in my Peninsula flat. Can I turn it off myself?",
      "a": "Usually, yes. There are normally valves on the pipes going into the unit and a separate cold water valve for the flat, and closing them stops most leaks from that cupboard. Put a tray or towels down, switch off the unit’s power if it has its own switch, and tell the building’s team, because the network side is theirs. If the leak carries on with everything closed, it is coming from the building’s pipework."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|chelsea": {
    "local": "In a Chelsea mews house, the ground floor was usually built for carriages or horses, so today the stopcock and meter are often in the garage or a front cupboard, with the living space above. Water from an upstairs bathroom has nowhere to go but down into that garage, onto any car or stored belongings. Turn it off there, then switch off power to the ground floor. In the tall townhouses, a booster pump or pumped shower may keep running after the mains is off, drawing on stored water, so switch those off at their own switches too. For night access, a mews can be gated, or cobbled with no room to turn a van, and some have a gate code or a keyholder. Tell us about any gate, and whether a neighbour or house manager can open up if you are not there yourself.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The garage on the ground floor of our mews house is flooding. Where is it coming from?",
      "a": "Most likely from above. In a mews house the bathroom and kitchen are over the garage, so a leaking supply, a failed waste joint or an overflowing bath shows up there first. Turn the stopcock off and see whether it slows. If it keeps coming with the water off, check whether rain is getting in, or whether the house next door has a bathroom against the shared wall."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|tooting": {
    "local": "Tooting’s added bathrooms are where emergencies tend to start. A shower room squeezed into a back addition or a loft often relies on a macerator toilet or a small pump to get waste to the stack. If one of those fails, stop using that room entirely and switch off its power at the switch nearby, since the unit will keep trying and can overflow. If toilets are backing up on the lower floor, ask everyone in the house to stop flushing and showering until the stack is cleared. In a house share, agree who will speak to the landlord and who will wait for the engineer. At night, the people who can let us in are sometimes working shifts or asleep in a back room, so send us a number for whoever will be awake. Near the Broadway, flats above shops often have a separate door beside the shopfront.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Water appeared on the floor after several of us showered one after another. Is it a leak?",
      "a": "Possibly not a supply leak at all. When a stack sized for one bathroom takes several showers in a row, waste can back up at the lowest joint or trap and seep out, then stop once the flow eases. Try running one outlet at a time and see where water appears. If it only happens under heavy use, it is a drainage fault that can be booked for daytime. If it keeps coming regardless, turn the water off and call."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|enfield": {
    "local": "Enfield’s winter emergencies tend to happen in the places nobody goes in the evening. A pipe that split in the garage during a freeze may only start leaking as it thaws, and the first sign is often water running out under the door and across the drive. If that happens, the stopcock is the priority, and on these plots it may be in that same garage, so keep the key somewhere you can find it. Never thaw a frozen pipe with a blowtorch or open flame. Gentle heat from a hairdryer or warm cloths is safer, used with the water off and kept well away from anything wet. Inside the house, check around the loft hatch and any pipe on an outside wall. For a night callout, the engineer will need the garage or outbuilding unlocked, a clear path to it and some light. On bigger plots, tell us whether to come to the front door or straight round the side.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "There is a wet patch on the lawn and our water pressure has dropped. Is that our pipe?",
      "a": "It may well be. On a long plot, the supply pipe from the boundary to the house runs under the garden, and older ones can split or corrode. If the wet patch is between the outside stop tap and the house, the pipe is usually the owner’s responsibility. If it is on the pavement side of the stop tap, report it to your water company instead. It is rarely a flood, so it can often wait for daylight if the pressure holds."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|barnet": {
    "local": "In the older houses around Chipping Barnet, the stopcock has often been covered by later work: a fitted kitchen, a new floor or a boxed-in cupboard under the stairs. If you cannot reach it quickly, go to the outside stop tap rather than pulling units apart. In the larger suburban houses towards New Barnet, extensions have usually added isolation valves over the years, often unlabelled, so a leak in the newest bathroom may have its own valve under the basin or behind a bath panel. Try that before shutting off the whole house. If the leak is in the loft, stay on the joists and use a torch rather than the loft light if water is near it. For night visits, flats over shops in the centre usually have a side or rear door rather than a street entrance. Out in the suburbs, tell us if the problem is in an annexe or garage so the engineer can head straight there.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "When I turned my water off, my neighbour lost theirs too. Is that normal?",
      "a": "In older Barnet houses it can be. Some period properties were connected on a single shared supply pipe that splits once it is inside, so one outside stop tap serves both. Turn it back on only once the leak is isolated inside your house, using an internal valve if you can find one. Longer term, a separate supply for each house avoids the problem, and your water company can advise on whether that is possible."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|guildford": {
    "local": "Because an engineer is travelling out from Fulham, what you do before they arrive matters more in Guildford than it would near our base. Turn the water off, switch off power to any wet rooms, move what you can out of the way and put containers under drips. If the stopcock has not been turned in years, expect it to be stiff, because the hard water here leaves scale in valves as well as in kettles. Turn it slowly, and if it will not budge, use the outside stop tap. In the villages around the town, many houses have names rather than numbers and sit on unlit lanes, so describe the gate, the nearest turning or anything visible from the road. Near the university, shared student houses can sit empty over the holidays, which is when a slow leak gets a head start. If you let one, a tenant who knows where the stopcock is saves a great deal.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Can you help if my oil boiler fails overnight?",
      "a": "Not with the boiler itself. We work on gas only, so an oil-fired boiler in one of the villages needs an oil heating engineer. What we can still help with is the plumbing around it: a burst pipe, a leaking cylinder or a radiator valve that has let go. If water is escaping, turn it off at the stopcock and call us for that part, and contact an oil engineer for the boiler."
    }
  },
  "emergency-plumbing|woking": {
    "local": "In the 1970s and 80s houses around Goldsworth Park and the other estates, the stopcock is usually under the kitchen sink, though extended kitchens sometimes hide it behind a newer cupboard. Many of these houses still have a cold water tank in the loft, so if water keeps coming after the mains is off, that is where it is coming from. Integral garages often carry pipes along an outside wall, and those are the first to freeze in a hard winter. In the bigger extended houses in Horsell and Pyrford, several bathrooms usually mean several local valves, and they are worth labelling. Because engineers come out from Fulham, use the waiting time well: water off, power off to wet rooms, valuables moved. At night the estates can be hard to find your way round, with culs-de-sac and shared drives, so tell us which way the house faces and what is next to it.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Water is running from the overflow pipe on our outside wall. Is that an emergency?",
      "a": "Usually not, but do not ignore it. A steady drip or run from an overflow normally means a float valve in the loft tank or a toilet cistern is not shutting off, and with hard water, scale on the valve is a common cause. Check the toilets first to see which one it is. If it is the loft tank, turning off the mains will stop it overnight. It becomes urgent if the tank or a cistern is overflowing inside."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|wimbledon": {
    "local": "Up towards the Village and the Common, the larger Wimbledon houses tend to have a regular boiler feeding a cylinder, with a cold water tank in the loft and a pump and motorised valves tucked into the airing cupboard. A breakdown in that setup is often one of those parts rather than the boiler: a sticking valve, a pump that seized while the heating was off over summer, or a programmer that has lost its settings. Nearer the station and the town centre, the Victorian terraces are largely flats with a combi in the kitchen, where the usual faults are low pressure, a failed diverter valve or a blocked condensate trap. Most of the bigger houses have a driveway, which makes carrying in a heavy part simpler than in inner London. Where a repair would change the flue on a period house near the Common, we check the conservation position before anything outside is altered.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The hot water works but the radiators stay cold. Where do I start?",
      "a": "In a Wimbledon house with a regular boiler and cylinder, working hot water tells you the boiler is lighting and heating water. The fault is more likely the motorised valve that sends heat to the radiators, its wiring, or the room thermostat not calling for heat. An airlock on an upper floor is another common cause in older layouts. We test the valve, pump and controls before touching the boiler, which usually keeps the repair small."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|ealing": {
    "local": "Ealing’s larger Edwardian and interwar houses were mostly built around a regular boiler, a loft tank and a cylinder, and many have since been changed piecemeal: a combi in one house, a system boiler with an unvented cylinder in the next, sometimes a second boiler added for a loft floor. Knowing which you have narrows the fault quickly, so a photo of the boiler and of whatever is in the airing cupboard helps before the visit. Ealing’s purpose-built blocks, many dating from the 1930s, are a different job. Flats there usually have a small combi in a kitchen or hall cupboard, and its flue may exit into a light well or onto a rear wall shared with neighbours. Swapping a part is simple; changing the flue can need the freeholder’s agreement first. Houses often have a driveway, while the blocks mean a lift or stairs and tighter parking, which affects how long a visit takes.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "My flat’s boiler flue opens into a light well shared with other flats. Is that allowed?",
      "a": "It can be, if the terminal keeps the required distances from windows, vents and surrounding walls, and the space is open enough for fumes to clear. Some installations in Ealing’s purpose-built blocks were fitted under older rules or squeezed in where space was short. On a repair visit we check the flue and terminal position. If it falls short of current standards, we explain what that means and who in the building needs to agree to any change."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|croydon": {
    "local": "Croydon’s 1930s semis and estates still hold a fair number of back boilers, fitted behind a gas fire in the front room when central heating first arrived. They can often be repaired, but some of their parts are no longer made, so the first job is finding out whether the part exists. Houses that have moved on usually have a combi in the kitchen or a system boiler with the cylinder kept upstairs. Above the shops in the town centre, the converted flats mostly run a compact combi in a cupboard, sometimes with a flue out through a flat roof or a shared rear wall, which brings the freeholder in if anything external has to change. The towers are another matter again, with communal plant that is the building’s responsibility. Croydon water is hard, so combis here scale up on their hot water heat exchanger. Suburban streets generally have room outside; near the centre it is car parks and loading bays.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I still have a back boiler behind the gas fire. Can you repair it?",
      "a": "Often, yes, if the failed part can still be sourced, such as a thermocouple, a gas valve or a control. Plenty of Croydon semis kept theirs when the rest of the house was modernised. The catch is that parts for some older back boilers are no longer made, and then repair stops being an option. We check availability before quoting, and if replacement is the only answer we explain what moving to a wall-hung boiler would involve."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|harrow": {
    "local": "Most Harrow semis were built with an airing-cupboard cylinder on the landing fed from a tank in the roof space, and a good number still work that way, now with a regular or system boiler in the kitchen or garage. When a loft conversion goes in, that loft tank is often moved or shrunk to make room, which can leave the system prone to airlocks and to water pumping over into the tank. Houses that switched to a combi at the same time have a different weak point: surviving galvanised mains pipe that restricts flow into the boiler, so hot water comes through weak or lukewarm. Boilers in garages are common here too, and their condensate pipe needs protecting, because an unheated garage freezes well before the house does. Driveways make access easy in most of HA1 to HA5; the loft hatch is usually the tight part of the visit.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Since the loft conversion the pump in the airing cupboard is noisy. Is the boiler failing?",
      "a": "Unlikely. Noise or gurgling after a loft conversion usually means air in the system, often because the small feed and expansion tank was moved or lowered during the building work. It is a common story in Harrow semis on a vented setup. We check the tank height, bleed the system and look at the pump speed. The boiler itself is usually fine, and we will say so rather than quote for a new one."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|fulham": {
    "local": "Fulham is our own patch, and most boiler calls here are combis serving one flat in a converted terrace. Each flat in a house may have its own boiler, gas meter and flue, and from inside it is not always obvious which terminal on the back wall is yours. Upper flats often drain their condensate into the shared soil stack, so a blockage lower down can stop it draining and lock the boiler out. Lower-ground flats have the opposite issue: the flue terminal sits close to windows, doors or a neighbour’s lightwell, which limits what can be changed. Terraces still used as one house more often have a system boiler and cylinder, sometimes moved into the loft or a cellar during a renovation. Leaseholders should know that altering a flue on a shared wall usually needs the freeholder’s consent. Most SW6 streets are permit parking, so give us any access notes when you book.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The flat below says steam from my boiler flue blows towards their window. Is that a fault?",
      "a": "Some visible plume from a condensing boiler is normal, especially on cold mornings. A terminal too close to a neighbour’s window or door is still worth checking, and in Fulham conversions flues were sometimes fitted wherever the wall allowed. We inspect the flue and its clearances, test how the boiler is burning, and tell you whether it is harmless vapour or needs a plume kit to direct it away. Changes on a shared wall may need the freeholder."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|wandsworth": {
    "local": "In Wandsworth’s extended terraces the boiler has often moved twice: from the original back addition into a side-return kitchen, and sometimes on up into the loft when the family wanted the cupboard space back. Each move lengthens the gas pipe to the boiler, and a pipe that was not upsized for the extra length can starve the burner. That shows up as ignition failures and lockouts on a boiler that otherwise tests fine, so we check the gas pressure at the appliance before blaming any part. Flats carved out of the same terraces generally have a combi each. The riverside new-build blocks tend to have sealed, pressurised systems, some served from a communal plant room, where building management has to arrange access and agree who pays. Street parking is mostly permit-controlled and front gardens are small, so we aim to bring everything in one trip.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our boiler was moved into the loft during the conversion. Is that harder to repair?",
      "a": "It can take longer, mainly because of access. A loft boiler needs a fixed ladder, boarding and lighting so it can be worked on safely, and some Wandsworth loft conversions left it behind a small hatch in the eaves. The faults themselves are the same as anywhere. Send a photo showing how the boiler is reached and we can tell you before the visit whether anything needs sorting first."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|clapham": {
    "local": "Most boiler repairs in Clapham happen in rented homes, and that shapes the visit as much as the boiler does. In a converted flat the unit is normally a combi; in a larger shared house, a landlord may have fitted a system boiler with a cylinder to cope with several showers. On a combi, heavy daily use wears the diverter valve and scales up the plate heat exchanger on the hot water side, because London water is hard. With a cylinder, tenants often fall back on the immersion heater, and that can hide a boiler fault for weeks until the electricity bill arrives. Practically, we need to know who is authorising the repair: the tenant who rang, the landlord, or a letting agent. Agree that before the visit and say who will be at home, so the engineer is not left on the doorstep. Parking on the residential streets is mostly permit-only.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I rent my flat and the boiler has broken. Can I book the repair myself?",
      "a": "You can, but speak to your landlord or letting agent first. The boiler is their responsibility, and they may already use someone or have a cover plan in place. If they ask us to go ahead, we take the details from you, agree the price with them, and send the paperwork to whoever is paying. In Clapham that three-way arrangement is common, and it goes smoothly when everyone knows who is doing what."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|bromley": {
    "local": "Bromley’s semis and detached houses are mostly on open-vented systems: a regular boiler, a feed tank in the loft, a cylinder on the landing and a pump with valves hidden in the airing cupboard. Some older setups still heat the cylinder by gravity, with only the radiators pumped, which behaves differently from a fully pumped system and fails differently too. Larger houses may have two heating zones, each with its own valve and thermostat, so one cold floor usually points at a zone rather than the boiler. The water here is hard, and years of fresh water entering through the loft tank leave scale in the boiler’s heat exchanger. Older boilers sit in kitchens, utility rooms and sometimes attached garages, and most homes have a driveway, so getting parts and tools in is rarely the difficulty. Loft access for checking the tank is worth clearing before we arrive.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "My boiler rumbles like a kettle when it fires. Is it dangerous?",
      "a": "Not usually an immediate danger, but it should not be ignored. The sound, known as kettling, normally means scale or sludge is restricting flow through the heat exchanger, so water overheats in patches. With Bromley’s hard water and older open-vented systems it is a familiar noise. Left alone it strains the heat exchanger and can end in a lockout. The fix may be a chemical clean, a flush or a new pump, and we find out which first."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|brixton": {
    "local": "In Brixton, who owns the boiler matters as much as what is wrong with it. On the estates, a tenant’s boiler is normally the landlord’s to repair, whether that is the council or a housing association, so their repairs line is the first call. A leaseholder in the same block usually owns the boiler inside the flat and arranges repairs privately, even though next door is maintained by the landlord. Some estates have no individual boilers at all and run on a shared heating network. In the converted Victorian terraces, each flat typically has its own combi, often in a kitchen cupboard, with a flue out of the back wall or up through the roof. Flats above shops on the main roads are often reached by a rear staircase or a side door, so tell us how to get in. Parking near the market is limited, and a side street is often quicker.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I bought my estate flat. Why won’t the council fix my boiler any more?",
      "a": "Once you own the lease, the boiler inside the flat is usually your responsibility, while the freeholder keeps the structure, the communal pipework and any shared plant. Your lease sets out the split and is worth reading. It catches out plenty of new owners after a right-to-buy purchase in Brixton. We repair the boiler itself, and if the fault turns out to be on the shared side, we write down what we found so you can report it."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|hackney": {
    "local": "Hackney’s warehouse and factory conversions often pair a combi or system boiler with underfloor heating, and the boiler may sit on a mezzanine or high on a wall where a flue could reach outside. Underfloor heating adds a manifold, a mixing valve and a separate pump between the boiler and the floor, and a cold floor is at least as likely to be one of those. High-level boilers also need safe access, so tell us if there is a ladder or steps we can use. In the Victorian terraces across E8, E5 and N16 that were split into flats, one combi per flat is the norm, often squeezed in during the conversion. Estate blocks are more mixed: some flats have their own boilers, others sit on shared plant the landlord maintains. Many residential streets have traffic filters and permit parking, so a full postcode and access notes help us plan.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "My underfloor heating is cold but the radiators work. Is the boiler at fault?",
      "a": "Almost certainly not, if the radiators are hot. The boiler is producing heat; it is just not reaching the floor. The usual suspects are the underfloor pump, the mixing valve, an actuator on the manifold, or the thermostat for that zone. In Hackney conversions those parts often live in a cupboard nobody has opened since the flat was finished. We test each one and replace only what has failed."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|dulwich": {
    "local": "Big Dulwich houses often keep the boiler in a cellar or basement utility room, and some older units there are open-flued, drawing combustion air from the room itself. That makes ventilation part of any repair. If a cellar has been tanked, sealed or turned into a gym, the boiler may no longer get the air it needs, and we will tell you. Many of these homes run a system boiler with a large cylinder and two or three heating zones, and a lot have had smart thermostats wired into older control centres. Faults in that wiring and in the zone valves are common, and easy to mistake for a boiler problem. Where a repair would move or add a flue terminal on the outside of the house, check whether the Dulwich Estate scheme of management covers your property before agreeing to it. Parking is rarely a problem on these roads.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "We fitted a smart thermostat and now the boiler behaves oddly. Is it broken?",
      "a": "Probably not. Smart thermostats are often wired into an existing control centre set up for a different kind of switching, and one small mistake can leave a zone valve open, the pump running on, or the boiler firing when nothing is asking for heat. In large Dulwich houses with several zones, that produces confusing symptoms. We check the wiring against the controls before assuming any boiler part has failed."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|greenwich": {
    "local": "Between the Georgian town centre and the Peninsula towers sits a lot of ordinary Greenwich housing: Victorian and interwar terraces heading east towards Charlton, many split into flats with a combi each. Those are straightforward repairs. The period houses closer to the park are less so. Some still have an older open-flued boiler venting into a lined chimney from a basement kitchen, and any repair there includes checking that the flue draws properly, because a blocked or broken liner is a carbon monoxide risk rather than a nuisance. Where a modern boiler has taken its place, the flue has often been taken out through a rear wall or a light well to keep it off the front elevation. On the Peninsula, repairs to heat interface units are usually booked through building management, who control access to risers and meters. Town-centre parking is tight, and on the Peninsula it is worth asking where contractors should park.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "My Georgian house has a boiler in the basement using the old chimney. Is that still safe?",
      "a": "It can be, if the chimney is lined, the liner is intact and the room has the air supply the boiler needs. We check all three during a repair visit and test the flue. If the boiler is very old, parts may be hard to find, and a replacement with a sealed flue could change what is allowed outside in a conservation area. We talk that through before anything moves. A carbon monoxide alarm in that room is sensible either way."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|kensington": {
    "local": "In Kensington’s deep basement conversions the boiler often lives in a plant room below the level of the drains, so its condensate has to be pumped up and away. When that small pump fails or clogs, the boiler locks out, and it looks like a boiler fault when it is not. Those plant rooms frequently hold a system boiler, sometimes two, with a large unvented cylinder serving several bathrooms. Stucco houses divided into lateral flats more commonly have a combi per flat, fitted in a hall cupboard or a ceiling void. Mansion blocks vary: some still take heating and hot water from central plant, while others have had individual boilers added flat by flat, each with its own flue arrangement. Many blocks have a porter who controls access and working hours, so let them know we are coming. Parking is residents’ bays and short-stay meters, and we plan the visit around that.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our block only lets contractors work certain hours. Will that slow the repair?",
      "a": "It can, which is why we ask about building rules when you book. Many Kensington mansion blocks limit noisy work and deliveries to set times, and some want insurance details before a contractor is let in. We are fully insured and can send the paperwork ahead. If a part has to be ordered, we book the return visit inside the permitted hours, so the building is not disturbed and nobody has to bend the rules."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|walthamstow": {
    "local": "Walthamstow’s terraces have mostly been through a round of modernisation, and the usual result is a combi where there was once a back boiler behind the front-room fire and a cylinder upstairs. Not every house made the switch, and a surviving back boiler is still a real repair question here, with parts for some older models hard to find. In the Warner maisonettes, each home generally has its own boiler, but the upper and lower homes share walls, floors and sometimes the rear wall where flues come out, so work on one flue can affect both households. The water is hard, which shows up as scale in the combi’s hot water heat exchanger and as temperature that swings at the tap. Much of E17 has controlled parking, and some residential streets have filtered traffic, so a clear postcode and a note about access help.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "My shower swings from hot to cold and back. What causes that?",
      "a": "On a combi, the usual causes are scale on the plate heat exchanger, a sticking flow sensor, or incoming water pressure dropping below what the boiler needs to stay lit. Walthamstow’s hard water makes the first of those common, particularly where no scale reducer was fitted on the mains. We check the incoming pressure and flow first, then the heat exchanger, and clean or replace whichever part is actually to blame."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|chelsea": {
    "local": "Tall Chelsea townhouses tend to keep the boiler and a large cylinder at the bottom of the house, in a basement or the vaults, with the bedrooms and bathrooms four or five floors above. Some have been converted to an unvented cylinder with a pump boosting flow up the stairs, so there are more parts to check before the boiler is blamed, and the circulating pump works harder than it would in a two-storey house. Mews houses are a separate job. Many started as stables or coach houses, the boiler sits in what was the garage or in a first-floor kitchen, and the back wall often adjoins another building, so the flue can only come out onto the mews. A van will not always fit down a narrow mews, and parking nearby is residents’ bays and meters, so allow for a short carry. Mansion block flats here are more often on a compact combi.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The top-floor radiators keep needing bleeding. Is the boiler letting air in?",
      "a": "Rarely the boiler itself. Air returning to a sealed system usually means a small leak, a failing automatic air vent, or a pressure problem that lets air be drawn in. On an older open system, the pump can push water up into the feed tank and pull air back. In a tall Chelsea house that air gathers on the top floor, so that is where you notice it. We look for the cause rather than just bleeding the radiators again."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|battersea": {
    "local": "Battersea’s boiler work falls into three kinds of home. Off the park, the Victorian terraces and mansion blocks mostly have a combi per flat, fitted when the buildings were divided up or modernised, with flues out of back walls and condensate taken into shared stacks. Between them and the river there are post-war estate blocks, where flats may have individual boilers or be on shared plant, and tenants there should report a boiler fault to their landlord. The newer towers at Nine Elms rarely have a boiler at all. If your flat there is recent enough to still be under the developer’s or builder’s cover, report the fault to them before paying anyone, because a failed part may be theirs to put right. In the terraces, parking is permit bays and short-stay meters, and upper-floor conversions mean carrying parts up several flights, which is worth knowing if you are waiting in for us.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Can you work in my Nine Elms building, or does it have to be the developer’s contractor?",
      "a": "Usually we can, as long as building management agrees and we follow their contractor rules, which often include booking a slot, showing insurance and signing in at the desk. Some developments ask residents to use a nominated contractor for anything touching the heat network. For equipment inside your own flat, the choice is generally yours. Check your lease or resident guide, or ask the concierge, and we will fit around what they require."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|tooting": {
    "local": "Tooting’s terraced houses are mostly on combis now, whether they are family homes, shared houses or split into flats, and the boiler usually sits in the back-addition kitchen or a bedroom cupboard. In shared houses several people may describe the same fault in different ways, so one clear contact and a photo of the display save a wasted trip. Rented homes here often have prepayment gas meters, and a boiler that will not light may simply have run out of credit, or the meter may have cut the supply after an interruption. Check the meter display before booking. Above the shops and restaurants on the main roads, flats are reached from the back or through a side door, and a flue close to a kitchen extract can upset how the boiler burns, so we check the terminal area as well as the boiler. Side streets are usually easier for parking than the Broadway.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The landlord says the boiler is fine but the back rooms stay cold. Who is right?",
      "a": "Possibly both. In Tooting terraces, rooms in the back addition are often at the end of a long pipe run, with radiators that were never balanced after later bedrooms and bathrooms were added. The boiler can be healthy while those rooms get very little flow. We check the boiler first, then balance the radiators and look at pump speed and valve settings, which often fixes it without a new part. Our findings can go to both of you."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|hammersmith": {
    "local": "Hammersmith has plenty of Victorian terraces split into flats, each with its own combi, and those are ordinary repairs. The blocks are where it gets specific. Some older mansion blocks still supply heating and hot water from a central plant room, so the flat has radiators and perhaps a cylinder or heat exchanger, but no boiler of its own. Some post-war blocks in the area were built with a shared flue, a vertical duct that several flats’ boilers connect to, and a boiler on one of those has to be a model designed for it, which matters if a repair turns into a replacement. For small offices and shops around the Broadway with a domestic-sized gas boiler, we can come early or late so staff are not disrupted. Parking near the Broadway is scarce and the residential streets are permit bays, so access notes help.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Can you repair the boiler in our small office without closing for the day?",
      "a": "Usually, yes. A small office or shop with a domestic-sized gas boiler is repaired the same way as a home, and most faults are diagnosed on the first visit. Around the Broadway we can often book an early start before staff arrive, or an evening slot after they leave. If a part needs ordering, we make the system safe and agree the return visit with whoever manages the premises, so the office keeps running in the meantime."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|lewisham": {
    "local": "Lewisham’s ex-council flats raise a particular boiler question. Many leaseholders bought theirs with whatever heating the council fitted decades ago, which can be a back boiler behind a gas fire, a warm-air heater in a hall cupboard blowing through ducts, or a combi added more recently. Each needs a different repair, and the older two can run into parts that are no longer made. Leaseholders own that equipment; tenants report faults to the council or housing association instead. In the Victorian and Edwardian terraces towards Ladywell, Hither Green and Catford, the stock is mostly combis in houses and flats, often on pipework that has outlived two boilers. The new towers in the town centre are on managed systems where building staff arrange access. Estate car parks are often residents-only, so tell us where a van can stop.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I have warm-air heating in my flat. Can you still repair it?",
      "a": "Often, yes. A gas warm-air unit is a gas appliance like a boiler, and common faults such as a failed fan, thermostat or ignition part can usually be put right. They still turn up in Lewisham’s older council-built flats. The difficulty is age: some models are long out of production and parts can be scarce. If yours cannot be repaired, we explain the options, which may include a combi and radiators, before you commit to anything."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|putney": {
    "local": "Away from the river, much of Putney is large Victorian and Edwardian family housing, and those houses often run a system boiler with an unvented cylinder, put in when the loft tank came out during a loft or bathroom upgrade. Faults there are as often the cylinder’s controls, valves or expansion vessel as the boiler. Terraces nearer the bridge are more commonly divided into two or three flats, each with its own combi. The riverside mansion blocks usually need a managing agent’s agreement for anything beyond a like-for-like part, and some ask for notice before contractors arrive. At Roehampton the estate blocks are largely on communal heating, and council tenants report faults to the council, while leaseholders with their own boilers arrange repairs themselves. Parking near the high street is difficult, but most residential roads have permit or short-stay bays.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "A pipe outside keeps dripping near where my cylinder is. Is that serious?",
      "a": "It needs looking at soon. On an unvented cylinder, that pipe carries water from the safety valves, and a steady drip usually means the expansion vessel has lost its charge or a valve is letting by. Many larger Putney houses switched to unvented cylinders when their loft tanks were removed. It is not the boiler, but the cylinder’s safety parts must work properly, so book it in rather than living with the drip."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|acton": {
    "local": "A lot of Acton’s older terraces have gained a rear or side extension, and when that happens the boiler flue is not always considered. The result can be a terminal that now sits under the new roof edge, next to an opening rooflight, or venting into a space that has since been enclosed, which is a safety problem rather than a tidy-up. We check the flue position on any repair where the house has been extended. Converted flats and shared houses in the Victorian streets typically have one combi doing a lot of work, often in a ground-floor kitchen or on a landing. In the newer blocks around North Acton, flats may have their own compact boiler or a heat interface unit, and building management controls access to risers and plant. Parking is permit-controlled across much of W3, with more space in the outer streets.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our builder boxed in the boiler flue during the kitchen extension. Is that a problem?",
      "a": "It can be. A flue needs clear space around its terminal and must not discharge under a roof, into a lean-to, or near an opening window or rooflight. Boxing it in can also hide joints that need inspecting. In extended Acton terraces this is a common side effect of the building work. We check the terminal position and the joints, and if the flue needs extending or moving we explain what that involves before starting."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|guildford": {
    "local": "Boiler repair in Guildford starts with a drive out from our Fulham base, so we book a set time and give you an honest arrival estimate in an emergency. Much of the work is in the suburbs: interwar and post-war houses in areas such as Merrow and Burpham often have a system boiler and cylinder, while smaller terraces near the station and in Stoke are mostly on combis. Shared student houses near the university tend to have one combi serving several tenants, and faults often surface at the start of term after the boiler has stood idle over summer. Flats above shops in the older centre can have boilers in awkward rear rooms with limited flue options because of listed status. In the villages, check you are on mains gas before calling, because we do not work on oil boilers. Most suburban homes have a driveway, which makes the visit simpler.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Why do student house boilers here seem to fail at the start of term?",
      "a": "A boiler left unused over summer can seize: the pump sticks, the diverter valve gums up with scale, and the condensate trap can dry out. Guildford’s hard water makes the sticking worse. When everyone moves back and runs hot water at once, the weak part gives up. Running the heating and hot water briefly every couple of weeks over summer helps, and the annual landlord gas safety check is a good moment to catch problems before term."
    }
  },
  "boiler-repair|woking": {
    "local": "Many 1970s and 80s estate houses around Goldsworth Park were plumbed with microbore, thin copper tubes feeding each radiator from a central manifold. It works, but it blocks easily once sludge builds up, and a modern combi fitted onto it can overheat and lock out when the flow is choked. Cleaning the system properly is often part of fixing the boiler. In Horsell and Pyrford, the bigger extended houses more often run a system boiler and cylinder, sometimes serving three or four bathrooms. Victorian and Edwardian houses near the centre and Maybury are more varied, and occasionally still have an old floor-standing boiler. Town centre apartments usually need the managing company’s say before work starts. Our engineers come out from Fulham, so we agree a set time with you, and hard water scale is a regular finding here as well.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Some radiators stay cold and the boiler keeps overheating. Are they linked?",
      "a": "Quite possibly. In houses plumbed with microbore pipe, common on Woking’s 1970s and 80s estates, sludge can block the narrow tubes to some radiators. Water then cannot carry heat away fast enough, the boiler overheats and shuts itself down, and the blocked rooms stay cold. Replacing the part that trips does not solve it. We check flow through the system first, and a clean or flush is often the real repair."
    }
  },
  "drain-unblocking|croydon": {
    "local": "Most drain calls in Croydon’s suburban streets start outside rather than in. A 1930s semi typically has a gully by the back door taking the kitchen sink and a downpipe, another at the front, and an inspection chamber somewhere along the side passage. Over the years many of those chambers have vanished under block paving or a new driveway, and finding the cover can take longer than clearing the blockage. Pairs of semis often share a single pipe for the last stretch to the sewer, and a blockage in that stretch can show at next door’s gully first. Gardens wash leaves and silt into open gullies every autumn, and kitchen fat sets hard where the waste drops in. Closer to the town centre it changes: flats above shops, rear service yards and waste pipes shared with the business below, where lifting a cover may first need agreeing with the shop or the managing agent.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The gully outside my kitchen overflows when it rains. Is the drain blocked?",
      "a": "Possibly, but look at the gully first. Lift the grid if you can and see whether water is sitting in it. If the gully is full and the next chamber along is empty, the blockage is in the short length between them, often fat and leaves. If the chamber is full as well, the problem is further down. A gully that only overflows in a downpour and then drains may simply be taking more roof water than it can cope with."
    }
  },
  "drain-unblocking|bromley": {
    "local": "Drain work in Bromley often means a long walk with the equipment. On detached and semi-detached plots the pipe can pass under a side passage, a garage, a conservatory added later and a patio before it reaches a chamber. Extensions built over an old run sometimes leave a chamber indoors under a floor, or lose it altogether. Some older houses send rainwater to a soakaway in the garden instead of the sewer, and a soakaway that has silted up over decades behaves like a blocked drain: water backs up at the downpipe and pools on the lawn. Big gardens also mean outbuildings, utility rooms and garden sinks tied into the main drain, each one another branch that can catch debris. Parking is rarely the difficulty here. The width of the side gate and the number of fences between the van and the chamber usually are, so it helps to know where your covers are when you call.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Water pools at the bottom of a downpipe, but the drains inside are fine. What is that?",
      "a": "Usually the rainwater side, not the foul drain. On older houses here the downpipe may feed a soakaway in the garden rather than the sewer, and soakaways clog with silt over the years until they cannot take a heavy shower. It can also be a blocked gully at the foot of the pipe. Running water and checking the chambers tells us which. A failed soakaway needs rebuilding or replacing, not clearing."
    }
  },
  "drain-unblocking|wimbledon": {
    "local": "In Wimbledon the first question on a drain job is often whose garden the chamber is in. Victorian terraces near the town centre tend to have one pipe running along the backs of several houses, and the nearest cover can be two gardens away, so clearing it may mean asking a neighbour to let us through. Converted houses add another layer: the lower-ground or ground-floor flat sits closest to the drain and floods first, even when the blockage started with the flats above. Up towards the Village and the Common, bigger houses with several bathrooms have many branches feeding one private drain, often old clay under a long front drive. Some period houses there still have original chambers with heavy covers nobody has lifted in years. Parking around the town centre is controlled, and during the tennis fortnight in early summer the roads near the grounds are busier and more restricted, which is worth mentioning when you book.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I live in the ground-floor flat and waste is coming up in my shower. Is it my fault?",
      "a": "Not necessarily. In a converted house the lowest outlet is where a blockage shows first, wherever it started. The flats above keep using water and it has nowhere to go but your shower or toilet. Ask the other flats to stop running water until it is cleared. Afterwards we can tell you where the blockage was and what caused it, which helps settle who pays under the lease."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|ealing": {
    "local": "Ealing’s bigger Edwardian and interwar houses often have a system or regular boiler rather than a combi, feeding a cylinder in an airing cupboard, and some still keep the boiler in a cellar or utility room with a long flue through the wall. That gives a service more to look at: the cylinder controls, the pump, the valves and the condition of the flue where it passes through old brickwork. In the purpose-built 1930s blocks the boiler is usually in the kitchen, and the flue terminal can sit close to a neighbour’s window or a shared balcony, which has to be checked against current clearance rules. There are also a lot of let flats in Ealing, and many landlords book the annual gas safety check and the service together, so the tenant only has to be in once. Parking near the Broadway is controlled, so a permit or a nearby bay is worth sorting out beforehand.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I have a hot water cylinder as well as a boiler. Does the service cover it?",
      "a": "The service covers the boiler. We look at the controls that link it to the cylinder and check the cylinder for obvious problems. An unvented cylinder, though, has its own annual check of its safety valves and expansion vessel, and that should be booked as a separate item. Tell us which type you have when you book, or send a photo of its label, and we will allow the time."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|croydon": {
    "local": "In Croydon’s interwar semis the boiler has moved around as often as it has been replaced. It might be in the kitchen, in a converted loft or in the garage, and the flue has moved with it each time. Many of those flues come out into the side passage, close to the neighbour’s wall or a fence, and part of a proper service is checking that the flue is sound, sealed and still clear of anything built since it went in, such as a lean-to or a carport. Garage boilers bring their own points, covered below. Nearer the town centre, a lot of the flats above shops are let, which means the landlord owes the tenant a gas safety check every year as well as a working boiler. Access to those flats is often by a rear door off a service yard, so tell us how the engineer gets in and who will be there.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The boiler is in the garage. Does that change anything?",
      "a": "Garage boilers are common on these semis and are serviced like any other, but two things matter. The engineer needs clear space around it, so move bikes and boxes stacked against it before the visit. And an unheated garage leaves the pipework and condensate pipe open to frost, so we check the lagging and where the condensate drains as part of the job, because a frozen one stops the boiler in cold weather."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|bromley": {
    "local": "Many larger Bromley houses still run a regular boiler with a cylinder and a feed and expansion tank in the loft. On that kind of system it is worth looking beyond the boiler casing: the loft tank should have a lid and no debris floating in it, and the pipes up there need lagging, because a frozen feed pipe stops the whole system. Some older houses still have an open-flued boiler, or a back boiler behind a gas fire, drawing its air from the room. Those need a flue spillage test and a look at the ventilation, and a room that has since had new windows fitted or its air bricks covered can turn a safe appliance into an unsafe one. Detached houses sometimes have the boiler in a utility room or outbuilding some distance from the cylinder. Parking is rarely a problem here, and the time on the visit tends to go on the loft and the flue rather than the drive.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I still have a back boiler behind the gas fire. Can you service it?",
      "a": "Yes, if it is safe to work on and parts are still available. Back boilers are old by now, so the checks matter more than usual: the flue, the chimney it uses and the air supply to the room. If we find something that makes it unsafe, we are required to tell you and may have to turn it off until it is put right. Whatever the result, a working carbon monoxide alarm in that room is worth having."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|harrow": {
    "local": "Loft conversions in Harrow’s 1930s semis often moved the boiler too, either into the eaves cupboard of the new room or into what is left of the roof space. An eaves boiler can be serviced, but the engineer needs the cupboard emptied and enough room to take the case off and put test equipment into the flue. Where the flue goes straight up through the roof rather than out through a wall, the terminal can only be checked from inside and by eye from the ground, so any damage to it needs a separate visit with ladders or scaffolding. Houses still on a cylinder have a pump, a motorised valve and a programmer sitting outside the boiler, and those are part of what gets looked at, because a stuck valve can look like a boiler fault. Most roads here have driveways or easy kerbside parking, which keeps the visit to the time the work actually takes.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our boiler is in the eaves cupboard of the loft conversion. What do I need to clear?",
      "a": "Take out everything stored in front of and around it, including boxes resting against the pipework. The engineer needs to kneel in front of it, get the cover off and put a probe in the flue. If the cupboard door is small or the boiler sits a long way back, let us know when you book so we allow the time. If the installer fitted vents to the cupboard, keep those clear too."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|wimbledon": {
    "local": "Wimbledon’s larger houses tend to keep the boiler somewhere out of the way: a basement, a utility room off the kitchen, or a plant cupboard added during a big refurbishment. Some of the biggest have two boilers, or a boiler plus a separate water heater, and each needs its own service and its own record. Those refurbished houses usually came with a system boiler, an unvented cylinder, a zone valve for each floor and smart controls, so the engineer checks how they work together, not only the boiler. Closer to the station the picture is more modest: a combi in a kitchen cupboard, often in a let flat, where the tenant needs to be home and the landlord needs the paperwork. Near the Common, conservation rules can limit where a flue on a period house is allowed to go, so a flue that needs changing is not always a quick fix.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "We have two boilers. Can both be serviced in one visit?",
      "a": "Yes, and it is usually easier that way. Tell us when you book so we set aside the time for both. Each boiler gets its own checks, its own record and its own entry in the service history in its manual, which matters when both are still inside warranty terms. If one boiler looks after the heating and the other the hot water, we check the controls that link them as well."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|greenwich": {
    "local": "Greenwich town centre houses are tall and narrow, and the boiler is often at one extreme: in a basement kitchen or in a top-floor cupboard under the roof. Older installations in those houses sometimes flue into an original chimney through a liner, and checking that flue properly matters more than on a boiler that vents straight through an outside wall. In listed buildings the flue was often placed where it was allowed rather than where it was easiest, so reaching the terminal can take some planning. Out towards Blackheath the houses are larger, with more floors of radiators for one boiler to serve, and towards Charlton there are terraces and ex-council flats with combis in kitchens. Plenty of flats across SE10 are let, and landlords here often book the gas safety check alongside the service. Parking near the town centre is tight and controlled, and weekends are busy with visitors, so weekday slots are simpler.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "My boiler flues into an old chimney. Is that still safe?",
      "a": "It can be, provided the chimney has a liner and is sound and clear, and the boiler is designed for that type of flue. On a chimney flue the service includes testing that fumes are drawing upwards rather than spilling back into the room. Birds’ nests, fallen brickwork and a corroded liner are the usual problems. If we find one, we explain what it means and what fixing it involves before anything else is done."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|lewisham": {
    "local": "Lewisham has a lot of ex-local-authority flats now owned by leaseholders, and that changes who books the service. Council or housing association tenants in the same block usually have their boiler checked by the landlord’s contractor, but a leaseholder’s boiler is their own, and nobody else is going to arrange it. Many of those boilers flue straight out onto a shared walkway or balcony, so the terminal guard and the distance to doors and windows are part of the check. Getting in can mean an entry phone, a locked stairwell and a lift that may or may not be working, and estate parking is often by permit only. In the Victorian terraces towards Ladywell and Hither Green, the boiler is normally found in the back addition, with its flue coming out into the narrow gap between the kitchen and next door’s wall, often close to their window, which is exactly where clearances get tested.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I bought my ex-council flat. Does the council still service my boiler?",
      "a": "Usually not. Once you own the flat on a lease, the boiler inside it is normally yours to maintain, even if the council still services boilers for tenants elsewhere in the block. Check your lease to be sure, as a few are worded differently. If it has been years since anyone looked at it because you assumed someone else was doing it, book a service before the heating goes on."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|wandsworth": {
    "local": "Side-return extensions are everywhere in Wandsworth’s terraces, and they cause a specific problem for boiler flues. A flue that once vented into an open side passage can end up under a glass roof, beside a new rooflight or inside a space now enclosed on three sides. That is one of the first things an engineer looks at here, because fumes need somewhere to go. In converted terraces, the upper flat’s boiler is often in a cupboard on the landing or up in the loft, and the lower flat’s is in the kitchen, with both flues on the same back wall. Many of these flats are let, so landlords tend to ask for the gas safety check and the service at one appointment. Most streets are in a controlled parking zone, so a visitor permit, or a clear note on where to park, saves time on the day.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "We built a side-return extension. Could that affect the boiler flue?",
      "a": "It can, and it is worth checking even if the builder said it was fine. There are minimum clearances between a flue terminal and openings, roofs and boundaries, and an extension can bring any of those closer. If the flue now discharges into a partly enclosed space or near a rooflight that opens, it may need extending or moving. We check this on a service and explain any change before quoting for it."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|brixton": {
    "local": "A lot of Brixton’s flats sit above shops and food businesses on the main roads, reached by a side door and a steep stair. Their boilers often flue out the back into a yard shared with extractor ducts, bins and air-conditioning units, and a flue that was clear when it went in can end up boxed in by later equipment. That makes the flue check more than a formality. In the Victorian terraces split into flats, most boilers are combis in kitchens or hallway cupboards, and many are in let flats where the landlord needs a gas safety record each year and the tenant has to be home for it. Estate flats are mixed: some have their own boiler, others take heat from a communal system, and it is worth knowing which before booking. Parking near the market and the main roads is tight, so an early slot helps.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I let out a flat here. Can the gas safety check and the service be done together?",
      "a": "Yes, and most landlords do it that way. They are different things: the gas safety check is the legal inspection of every gas appliance and flue in the property, with a record you must give the tenant, while the service is maintenance on the boiler itself. One visit covers both if you book them together. Tell us about any gas hob or fire as well, because the check includes those."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|islington": {
    "local": "In Islington’s Georgian and early Victorian terraces the boiler is often at the top of the house, in a top-floor flat reached by four or five flights of shared stairs with no lift. Its flue may go up through the roof or out through a rear wall that cannot be altered because the building is listed, so the position it has now is usually the position it keeps. That makes the flue check matter: on a long or vertical flue, every joint and seal along its length needs to be sound, not just the terminal. Many owners here book the same slot every year, which suits a building where visits are arranged around neighbours who share the front door. Parking is almost all controlled and pay-by-phone, and the engineer carries tools and test equipment up those stairs, so a note about the nearest bay genuinely helps.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The engineer said my flue is hidden in the ceiling. What does that mean?",
      "a": "Some flues pass through a ceiling or roof void before reaching the outside, which is common in top-floor flats in converted period houses. The joints along that length need to be seen, so there should be inspection hatches in the right places. Without them the engineer cannot confirm the flue is sound and has to record that. Fitting hatches is a small job, and afterwards every service can check the whole flue."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|walthamstow": {
    "local": "Walthamstow’s terraces and Warner maisonettes are small enough that the boiler is usually in the kitchen or a cupboard off the landing, but the building work that has changed so many of them leaves its mark. A boiler moved during a kitchen knock-through often ended up in a tall cupboard with its pipework boxed in, which makes the case harder to get off. In an upper Warner maisonette the boiler may be in the loft, reached by a hatch and a ladder, and needs safe access before it can be serviced. Lower maisonettes and ground-floor flats sometimes have a flue coming out at head height beside a front path or passage, and that should have a guard fitted. Plenty of E17 flats are let to sharers, so landlords often want the annual gas safety record done on the same visit. Parking is controlled around the market and the station, and easier further out.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our upper maisonette boiler is in the loft with no light. What do we need to do?",
      "a": "The engineer needs a safe way up and somewhere safe to kneel. That means a fixed or sturdy loft ladder, a boarded floor between the hatch and the boiler, and a working light. Those are standard requirements for a boiler in a loft, not extras. If any are missing, tell us when you book: we can price adding them first, or the service may have to wait until they are in."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|streatham": {
    "local": "In Streatham’s divided Victorian and Edwardian houses there can be four or five boilers in one building, and their flues often come out on the same side or rear wall. Each gets its own service, but the engineer also looks at where the plume from one flat’s flue ends up, because a flue sitting under another flat’s opening window is a problem for the neighbour rather than the owner. In the 1930s mansion blocks, kitchens often face a light well or a rear courtyard, and flues discharging into that enclosed space need particular care. Leaseholders in those blocks usually own their boiler, while the managing agent looks after the building around it, including any shared flue or duct. Parking on and near the High Road is difficult, and the side streets towards the Common are usually easier.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "My neighbour’s flue is right under my window. Should I be worried?",
      "a": "Not automatically, but it is worth having checked. Flue terminals have to sit a set distance from windows, doors and air vents so fumes cannot find their way into a home. If you smell fumes, see staining around the window or a carbon monoxide alarm goes off, treat it as an emergency and ring the gas emergency line. Otherwise mention it to the neighbour or the managing agent and ask for it to be looked at on their next service."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|tooting": {
    "local": "Tooting has a high share of shared houses and let flats, which puts the landlord at the centre of most boiler services here. A shared house that needs a licence normally has to produce an up-to-date gas safety record each year, and the letting agent usually holds the keys and books the visit. Tenants tend to notice the symptoms first, such as a boiler that keeps losing pressure or hot water running cold when several showers go at once, and that detail is worth passing on, because the service is a good time to look at it. In the terraces with loft conversions the boiler is often in a top-floor cupboard, and the flue can come out right above a neighbour’s rear extension. Flats above the Broadway shops are reached by side doors and stairs, and parking near the Broadway is tight, so side streets further out are the usual answer.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Do rented rooms need a carbon monoxide alarm near the boiler?",
      "a": "In England, landlords must fit a carbon monoxide alarm in any room of a rented home that contains a fixed appliance burning fuel, which includes a gas boiler. Gas cookers are the exception. In a shared house that usually means the kitchen, or wherever the boiler sits. We can check the alarm is there and working during the service, but fitting it, and testing it at the start of each tenancy, is the landlord’s job."
    }
  },
  "boiler-service|acton": {
    "local": "Acton’s older terraces and conversions tend to have combis in kitchens or in a cupboard on the landing, and plenty of them are let, often to sharers. That means most services here are booked by a landlord or agent, with a tenant letting the engineer in, and the gas safety record and the service done in the same visit. Where the boiler was moved into a rear extension, the flue and the condensate pipe sometimes ended up longer than the manufacturer allows, or with extra bends added, which is worth checking against the installation instructions. Towards Acton Green and the W4 edge the houses are larger, with some system boilers and cylinders to look at alongside the boiler. Parking in the controlled zones near the stations is by permit or pay-by-phone, while streets further from the centre are easier, and engineers come across from Fulham.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The boiler was moved during our extension. How do I know it was done properly?",
      "a": "Ask for the paperwork first. A boiler moved by a Gas Safe engineer should have been notified, and you should have a Building Regulations compliance certificate for it. On the next service the engineer checks the flue length and bends against the manufacturer’s limits, the condensate pipe, and whether the flue is still clear of openings. If something is wrong, we explain it and price the fix before touching anything."
    }
  },
  "leak-detection|croydon": {
    "local": "Leak work in Croydon splits along the same lines as the housing. In the interwar semis the supply pipe from the pavement is often old, sometimes lead or iron, and sometimes shared with the house next door, so a leak on it can concern two households at once. Inside, ground floors are usually suspended timber, which helps: the heating pipes run in the void underneath, and a leak there can be traced and reached by lifting a board or two. Kitchen extensions changed that, with pipes buried in a concrete slab where a pinhole leak shows up as a boiler that keeps losing pressure. In flats above shops near the town centre, the first sign is often water coming through the shop ceiling below, and the shop, the flat owner and the managing agent all want to know whose pipe it is. Tracing it comes before anyone starts cutting.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "We share a water supply pipe with next door. Who pays if it leaks?",
      "a": "It depends on where the leak is and what your deeds say. Where one pipe feeds both houses, the length serving only your house is usually yours, the shared length is usually a joint responsibility, and the part under the public pavement is normally the water company’s. Tracing the exact point first avoids an argument. Some water companies help replace shared lead supplies with separate ones, so it is worth asking yours."
    }
  },
  "bathroom-installation|croydon": {
    "local": "Most interwar semis in the CR postcodes have the bathroom at the back of the first floor, over the kitchen, with the soil stack fixed to the outside rear wall. That stack is often original cast iron, and a refit is the sensible time to replace the section you connect into rather than joining new plastic onto a pipe that may be cracked. If the house has a textured ceiling or old vinyl floor tiles laid before 2000, have them tested before anyone strips them out, because some contain asbestos. Driveways are common on these streets, which makes deliveries much easier, and a skip standing on your own drive needs no council permit. Town centre flats are a different job. Materials come up a shared stair or a lift that may need booking, the rubble goes down the same way, and the building manager usually has a say in when noisy work can happen.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our bathroom and separate toilet are side by side. Can they become one room?",
      "a": "In a lot of 1930s semis, yes, and it is one of the most common changes people make. The wall between them is usually a lightweight partition, but we check before anything comes down. The WC can often stay on its existing soil connection, which keeps costs sensible, and the extra floor space makes room for a proper shower or a longer bath. The trade-off is losing a separate toilet, which matters more in a busy family house."
    }
  },
  "bathroom-installation|bromley": {
    "local": "Bromley houses tend to be generous, so the brief here is often bigger than a straight swap: a freestanding bath, a double basin, or a walk-in shower where the airing cupboard used to be. Each of those runs into the same limit on a vented system, which is how much hot water the cylinder actually stores. A large bath can drain a small cylinder before it is full. Taking out or shrinking the airing cupboard also means deciding where the cylinder goes, and that is a heating question as much as a bathroom one. On detached and semi-detached plots, access is rarely the problem. There is usually a drive for the van and for deliveries, and a side gate for getting old fittings out without carrying them through the hall. Where the new room sits some way from the existing stack, gravity waste is always the first choice and a pumped unit the fallback.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Will our hot water cylinder fill a large freestanding bath?",
      "a": "It depends on the size of the cylinder and the bath, and it is worth checking before you buy. A deep freestanding bath holds far more than a standard one, and on an older vented system the cylinder may run cold before the bath is full. The options are a larger cylinder, one that reheats faster, or a smaller bath. We look at what is in your airing cupboard when we visit and tell you plainly which applies."
    }
  },
  "bathroom-installation|harrow": {
    "local": "Harrow’s 1930s semis often still have galvanised steel pipes somewhere in the bathroom, usually under the bath or behind old panelling. Galvanised pipe rusts from the inside, narrowing the bore and discolouring the water, so once the floor is up it makes sense to replace whatever you can reach. The hot water cylinder is normally in an airing cupboard next door, and it may be the original copper one with an immersion heater. Family houses here also bring requests for a ground-floor shower room, typically in a rear extension, for an older relative or a growing household. That needs a waste connection at ground level and a floor built to take a level-access tray. For deliveries, most Harrow semis have a front drive and side access. The awkward part is indoors: stairs of this era are narrow with a tight turn, so check that a one-piece shower tray or long bath will get round it.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The pipes under our bath are grey metal. Should they be replaced during the refit?",
      "a": "Probably. Grey threaded pipe in a semi of this age is almost certainly galvanised steel, and it corrodes inside where you cannot see it. With the floor and bath panel already off, replacing it costs far less than coming back to it later. We show you what we find once the old suite is out, and we price any extra pipework before doing it, so the decision is yours rather than a surprise on the final bill."
    }
  },
  "bathroom-installation|wimbledon": {
    "local": "Nearer the station and the town centre, Wimbledon’s Victorian terraces usually have the bathroom in the back addition, above the kitchen. That room has three outside walls and a timber floor, so it is often the coldest in the house. A refit is the time to insulate the floor and any exposed wall, and to size the towel rail or radiator for the room rather than for looks. Timber floors also flex, which matters for tiles: large-format porcelain needs a stiff, well-fixed base or the grout lines crack. In the bigger family houses further up the hill, a new bathroom has to share hot water with the others already there, so balancing flow is part of the design. Parking is easier out there, with drives on many plots. Near the tennis club, the Championships bring extra restrictions every summer, which is worth knowing before you book a strip-out.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Can we have a wet room in the back-addition bathroom?",
      "a": "Often, yes, but a timber floor above a kitchen needs more care than a concrete one. The floor has to be stiffened, laid to a fall towards the drain, and fully tanked so nothing can reach the ceiling below. The drain position depends on which way the joists run, because the waste pipe has to go between them. We look under the floor before promising a layout, and tell you if a low shower tray would be the safer choice."
    }
  },
  "bathroom-installation|fulham": {
    "local": "Fulham conversions often put the bathroom in the middle of the flat with no window, so ventilation has to be designed in rather than left to an opening sash. The extractor needs ducting to the outside, which in a flat usually means a run through the ceiling to an external wall or up to the roof. Lower-ground flats bring a different problem. If the new basin or WC sits below the level of the drain it joins, the waste has to be pumped, and that pump needs a spot where it can be reached for servicing. The soil stack is normally shared with the flats above, so any work on it is timed so neighbours are not left without a toilet. Most Fulham streets are permit parking, though our vans start the day on the High Street and rarely come far. Carrying a bath up a shared hallway means protecting the common parts first.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our bathroom has no window. What ventilation does it need?",
      "a": "An extractor fan, ducted to the outside rather than into the ceiling void or loft. A bathroom without an opening window needs mechanical extraction, and a fan that simply vents into a void moves the damp somewhere you cannot see it. A fan that keeps running for a while after the light goes off clears steam properly. If the duct needs a new grille on the outside wall, check the lease, because that counts as an external alteration."
    }
  },
  "bathroom-installation|enfield": {
    "local": "Enfield’s interwar and post-war semis often have a bathroom over an integral garage or a porch, which means a cold floor and pipes crossing unheated space. A refit is the time to insulate beneath the floor and keep supply pipes on the warm side of that insulation, so a hard frost does not split them. Older houses around Enfield Town may still have a cast iron soil stack on the back wall, and connecting a new WC into a cracked one is a false economy. Big plots help with the practical side. Most houses have a drive, so a skip can sit off the road without a permit and the van can park close to the door. If the airing cupboard is being given up for a larger shower, the cylinder needs a new home first, and in a semi with a small loft that decision can shape the whole layout.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "We want a shower room in our garage conversion. Can the waste reach the drain?",
      "a": "Usually, and gravity is always the first option. On a large Enfield plot the nearest inspection chamber may be some distance away, so we check its depth and position before you fix the layout. If the fall is not there, a pumped waste unit is the fallback, with power and access for servicing. The conversion itself will involve building control, so it helps to settle the drainage before those drawings are finished."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|kensington": {
    "local": "Much of Kensington is residents’ permit parking, and on a busy street the van may need a suspended bay to unload a boiler, flue kit and cylinder. Suspensions are booked through the council in advance, so that goes on the list early. In mansion blocks, the porter or managing agent usually wants notice of deliveries, a booked lift, and protection laid in the common parts before anything is carried through. Stucco terraces with a front lightwell sometimes keep the boiler down there, in one of the old vaults under the pavement. Those are cramped, damp spaces, and the flue has to come out into the lightwell without discharging near a window or door. Once the boiler is in, the Gas Safe engineer notifies building control for you. The compliance certificate that arrives afterwards belongs with your deeds or lease papers, because solicitors ask for it when the property is sold.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Do I need planning permission for a new flue on a Kensington house?",
      "a": "If the building is listed, changes to the outside, including a new flue terminal, generally need listed building consent. In a conservation area, a flue on a wall facing the road may need planning permission, while one at the back often does not. Replacing a boiler with the terminal in the same place is usually simpler. Check with the council’s planning team before booking, and we will give you the flue details they will ask for."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|ealing": {
    "local": "Many of Ealing’s large Edwardian houses still run an old heat-only boiler with a cylinder and two tanks in the loft. Replacing that with a system boiler and an unvented cylinder frees the loft and gives mains pressure at every tap, but only if the incoming supply can deliver it. Houses of this age sometimes still have a narrow lead supply pipe from the street, and that caps what any new setup can do. We measure flow and pressure at the kitchen tap before recommending anything. In the 1930s purpose-built flats, you own the boiler but not the wall it flues through, so a changed terminal position needs the freeholder’s agreement. Parking near the Broadway is mostly permit-controlled; further out, front drives make delivery easy. Either way, the Gas Safe engineer registers the job with building control, so there is no separate application to make.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Can we get rid of the tanks in the loft when the boiler is replaced?",
      "a": "Usually, if the mains supply is good enough. Going to a system boiler with an unvented cylinder, or a combi in a smaller house, removes the need for loft tanks. The catch in older houses is the incoming pipe. If flow at the kitchen tap is poor, an unvented setup will be poor too, and upgrading the supply pipe is a separate job. We test it first so you know which way to go."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|wandsworth": {
    "local": "Many Wandsworth conversions were done a generation ago, and the boilers the converters fitted are now wearing out at roughly the same time. In an upper flat, check where the condensate pipe from the current boiler ends up. It often runs out through the wall and drips onto the lower flat’s extension roof, which stains the roof covering and can freeze solid in a hard winter, shutting the boiler down. A new installation should take the condensate to an internal waste pipe wherever it can. Towards the river, the newer blocks usually have pressurised systems or communal heating, and any change goes through building management. Most of the borough’s residential streets have permit parking, so a visitor permit or bay suspension helps on the day. Replacing a boiler is notifiable work, and your Gas Safe installer handles that notification rather than you applying to the council yourself.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our extension was built around the old boiler flue. Is that a problem?",
      "a": "It can be a serious one. A flue that now discharges into a side return that has been roofed over, under a glazed roof, or close to a new rooflight may no longer be safe, because fumes can collect where they should disperse. If you think yours has been boxed in this way, have it checked now rather than waiting for the replacement. Any new boiler will need a flue position that meets current rules."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|wimbledon": {
    "local": "The larger Wimbledon houses often have a vented cylinder that has already outlived several boilers. Before replacing the boiler, it is worth checking the cylinder’s condition and its coil, because a new boiler paired with a tired, slow cylinder still gives slow hot water. The choice is between keeping the vented setup with its loft tanks or moving to an unvented cylinder, which depends on the mains supply and where a larger cylinder could sit. The flats nearer the town centre are simpler and usually suit a combi. Period houses near the Common may be in a conservation area, so a new flue on a front or side wall facing the road needs checking with Merton’s planning team before the position is fixed. Most family houses up the hill have a drive, which makes the day easier, and the old boiler and cylinder can go straight out to the van.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Do the radiators and pipework have to be replaced along with the boiler?",
      "a": "Not usually. What matters is the condition of the water inside the system. Older systems in big houses carry years of sludge, and fitting a new boiler onto that shortens its life. The system should be cleaned, dosed with inhibitor, and fitted with a filter as part of the installation. Radiators that stay cold at the bottom after cleaning may be worth replacing, and we point those out before quoting rather than afterwards."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|fulham": {
    "local": "Fulham’s flat conversions very often have the combi squeezed into a kitchen cupboard, and a replacement is a chance to check it has the clearances needed for servicing. Where the flue comes out matters to the neighbours. A terminal below the upper flat’s window, or close to a shared side return, sends steam where people notice it, and a plume kit can move the discharge away. Any new hole through the outside wall usually needs the freeholder’s agreement first. Our base is on the High Street, so the van is never far away, but most streets are permit parking and a visitor permit or bay suspension makes loading easier. The new boiler goes up a narrow shared staircase and the old one comes down it, so the common parts are covered first. The engineer who fits it notifies building control, and the compliance certificate is posted to you afterwards.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our gas meter is in the communal hall downstairs. Does that affect a new boiler?",
      "a": "It can. The pipe carrying gas up to the flat must be sized for the new boiler, and a modern combi draws more gas at full output than many older boilers. A long run up through a converted house may be too narrow, which shows up as poor hot water. We check the pressure at the boiler with it running. If the pipe needs upgrading through communal areas, the freeholder may need to agree."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|croydon": {
    "local": "A lot of Croydon’s interwar semis still have a conventional boiler fed by loft tanks, with its cylinder in the airing cupboard. Changing to a combi frees the airing cupboard and the loft, but it also removes stored hot water, so it suits a smaller household better than a big one. On a semi outside a conservation area, the flue can usually go on the side or rear wall without planning questions, though the terminal still has to sit far enough from the boundary and from the windows next door. Driveways are common, which makes delivery simple. Nearer the town centre it is mostly flats above shops, where the gas meter may be in the shop or a rear lobby and access is often through a yard at the back. If you go combi, the old loft tanks should be drained and taken out rather than left sitting full above the ceiling.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Do we have to tell the council about a new boiler?",
      "a": "Not yourself. A boiler installation is notifiable under the Building Regulations, and the Gas Safe engineer who fits it notifies building control through the Gas Safe scheme. A compliance certificate is then posted to you. Planning permission is a separate question, and for a semi outside a conservation area it rarely arises. In a leasehold flat, check the lease for the freeholder’s consent before any change to the outside wall."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|bromley": {
    "local": "Bromley’s detached and larger semi-detached houses are among the easiest in London for flue positions: there is usually a side or rear wall well clear of windows and the boundary. Plenty are still on gravity-fed hot water from a loft tank and cylinder, and with several bathrooms that stored hot water is worth keeping. In a big house, controls matter as much as the boiler. One thermostat and timer for the whole house wastes gas heating rooms nobody uses, so separate zones for upstairs and downstairs, with thermostatic valves on the radiators, are worth fitting while the system is drained. Delivery is rarely difficult, with drives on most plots and plenty of room to work. Garages and outbuildings are the local quirk, because a boiler out there sits in unheated space. After commissioning, the installer registers the new boiler with building control.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our boiler lives in the garage. Can the new one go there too?",
      "a": "Usually, yes. A garage boiler needs frost protection, and the condensate pipe is the part most likely to freeze, so it should run inside or be sized and insulated for outdoor use. The pipes crossing the garage should be lagged, or you pay to heat it. If you are thinking of converting the garage later, decide that first, because moving a boiler after the conversion costs more than placing it right now."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|harrow": {
    "local": "Harrow’s semis are close together, with a narrow side passage between each pair. A flue terminal on that side wall ends up near the neighbour’s windows and the boundary, which limits where it can go and sometimes means a plume kit or a move to the rear wall. Many of these houses still store hot water in a copper cylinder in the airing cupboard, often with an immersion heater as backup. Heating systems on their third boiler usually carry a mix of old steel and newer copper, and that mix produces sludge. A thorough flush and a magnetic filter protect the new boiler from what the old pipework sheds. Front drives make delivery straightforward on most streets. Building control is notified by the Gas Safe engineer when the job is finished, and the compliance certificate arrives by post.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "We rely on the immersion heater when the boiler breaks down. Would we lose that with a combi?",
      "a": "Yes. A combi heats water as you use it, so there is no cylinder and no immersion heater to fall back on. If the boiler fails, you have no hot water until it is fixed. A system boiler with a cylinder keeps the immersion as a backup, at the cost of the airing cupboard space. For a household that has come to depend on that backup, it is a real reason to keep a cylinder."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|battersea": {
    "local": "The Victorian conversions in the streets off the park are where boiler installations actually happen in Battersea. The top flat’s boiler is often in the loft or a cupboard on the landing, and the new unit has to come up a steep shared staircase while the old one goes down it. Many of these flats are let, so the day has to be agreed with tenants, who will be without heating and hot water while the work is done. The lower flats may have a lightwell or garden wall for the flue, but a new terminal near the upper flat’s windows needs care. The red-brick mansion blocks along the edge of the park have their own rules, set by the managing agent. Parking is permit-controlled across most of SW11, and a bay suspension helps on a narrow street. The engineer notifies building control, and you receive the certificate.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I let out my flat. What paperwork should I have after the installation?",
      "a": "The Building Regulations compliance certificate, which follows once the engineer has notified the work, and the manufacturer’s commissioning checklist, filled in on the day. Keep both with the property records. A new installation does not replace the annual gas safety record your tenants are entitled to, so many landlords book the next safety check to line up with the installation date, which keeps the paperwork simple from then on."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|chelsea": {
    "local": "Chelsea’s mews houses usually have the boiler on the ground floor, in what was once the garage or stable, with the flue coming straight out onto the mews. A terminal at that height, where people walk past, needs a guard, and it must sit clear of the front door and any opening window. The mews itself can be the harder part. Some are private, some are cobbled and narrow, and a van may not get in at all, so the boiler and cylinder are carried from the nearest bay. Residents’ parking covers most of the borough, so bay suspensions need booking ahead. For listed houses, the flue position and any new opening need listed building consent before the installer can commit to a date. The Gas Safe notification to building control happens after the work, and it is quite separate from any consent the council gives.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Can the hot water cylinder go in the basement of our townhouse?",
      "a": "Often it is the best place for space, but hot water then has a long way to travel to the top floor, and you wait at the tap while it arrives. A secondary return, a small loop with its own pump, keeps hot water moving so it comes through quickly. Getting a large cylinder down a narrow basement stair also needs planning, so we measure the access before choosing the size."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|clapham": {
    "local": "Clapham has a lot of rented flats and shared houses, and a boiler installation in a let property is largely a scheduling job. Tenants need proper notice, and a day without heating or hot water is easier to agree in spring or summer than in January. Between tenancies is simplest of all. Heavy use means the hot water side deserves more thought here than the heating side, because it is the evening showers that expose an undersized system. In a flat where the kitchen and living room are one space, boiler noise matters, and a replacement is the time to check the unit is fixed to a solid wall. Lambeth’s residential streets are mostly permit parking, so a visitor permit for the van helps. Once the installation is commissioned, the engineer registers it with building control via Gas Safe and hands over the commissioning checklist.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I am a landlord. Can the installation be done between tenancies?",
      "a": "Yes, and an empty flat is the easiest case. We can visit while the current tenants are still in, agree the boiler and position, and fit it once they have left. If the flat is occupied, give your tenants written notice, agree a date that suits them, and make sure they know heating and hot water will be off for the day. Either way, we will want access to the meter and the flue position."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|brixton": {
    "local": "Many Brixton flats sit above shops, bars and restaurants, and the gas meter for the flat may be in the shop, a rear lobby or a shared cupboard at street level. Reaching it needs the business’s cooperation, and so does any work on the pipe that leaves it. At the back, flue terminals have to stay clear of commercial kitchen extract ducts and air intakes, which crowd many yards here. The main roads carry red lines where a van cannot stop to unload, so deliveries often come in from a side street or a rear service entrance. On the estates, a council or housing association tenant should not arrange a boiler replacement at all, because the landlord is responsible for it. Building control hears about the installation through the Gas Safe scheme, not from you.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "I own the lease on an ex-council flat. Who do I need to ask?",
      "a": "Usually the council or housing association that still holds the freehold. Most leases require their consent for alterations such as a new flue terminal, and some blocks have rules about where terminals can go. Get that consent in writing before booking a date. We can give you the details they tend to ask for: the boiler model, the flue position and what is being changed on the outside wall."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|greenwich": {
    "local": "The Georgian and early Victorian houses around Greenwich town centre often had open-flued boilers venting into an old chimney. Modern boilers are room-sealed, so the new flue usually needs a fresh position, and in a conservation area the front of the house is the least likely place to be allowed. A rear wall, or a vertical flue through a back roof slope, is the usual answer. Listed houses need listed building consent for new openings. Up towards Blackheath the houses are larger, and correct sizing for a big, draughty period property matters more than in a flat. The town centre is busy with visitors and parking is tight, so a bay suspension for the van is worth arranging. Once the job is finished, the Gas Safe engineer notifies building control, which covers the regulations side but not planning or listed building consent.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "The last boiler was fitted without any paperwork. Does that matter now?",
      "a": "It may mean the work was never notified to building control, which can come up when you sell. A new installation fixes that going forward: it is notified properly and you receive a compliance certificate. Older unrecorded work is a separate matter to raise with the council if a buyer’s solicitor asks. In older Greenwich houses with years of undocumented alterations, it is worth keeping every certificate from now on."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|putney": {
    "local": "Putney’s mansion blocks near the river and the bridge often have kitchens that look only onto an internal lightwell, shared by several flats with their own flues. Adding or moving a terminal there has to allow for the windows and flues already around it. Deliveries to an upper floor without a lift mean carrying the boiler up the common stair, which the agent will want protected. Up the hill in SW15, the Edwardian and interwar houses are larger and simpler, with front drives and flue positions on a side or rear wall. Parking near the High Street and the embankment is tight and mostly permit-controlled. Out at Roehampton, check first whether your heating is individual or communal. The engineer notifies building control for you once the job is complete, and the certificate is worth passing to your managing agent.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our lower-ground flat has pumped drainage. Where does the boiler condensate go?",
      "a": "Condensate is mildly acidic water and needs to reach a drain. Below drain level it can join the existing pumped waste if that pump is suitable for it, or go through a small condensate pump of its own. Either way it needs a power supply and occasional cleaning, and the pipe must not run anywhere it could freeze. We look at what is already there before choosing the boiler position."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|tooting": {
    "local": "Tooting houses change a lot over a few years. Families buy a terrace, add a loft room, then extend the kitchen, and each stage adds radiators and hot water demand. If you plan to extend, tell us when we quote, because the boiler should be sized for the finished house rather than the current one. The boiler itself is often in the back-addition kitchen, flued into a side return shared with the house next door, where the terminal has to keep its distance from their windows. For flats above the Broadway shops, deliveries have to fit around trading and a shared street door. Parking across SW17 is mostly permit-controlled, with busy roads near the station. Terraces rarely have side access, so the old boiler and any redundant tanks come out through the front door. The Gas Safe engineer registers the work with building control, and a certificate follows by post.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "We have no airing cupboard. Where would a hot water cylinder go?",
      "a": "In a terrace, the usual options are the loft, a cupboard built on the landing, or under the stairs if the gas meter leaves room. A loft cylinder needs a strong, boarded base and proper access. Wherever it goes, it has to be reachable for servicing and near enough to the bathrooms that you are not waiting for hot water. We look at the house before suggesting a system that needs one."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|balham": {
    "local": "Balham’s upper and lower flats each tend to have their own boiler. The upper flat’s is often in the loft or on the top landing, while the lower flat’s usually sits in the back-addition kitchen. When both flues come out at the back, one can end up blowing steam across the other’s window, so terminal positions are best thought about as a pair. The loft may not be part of the upper flat’s lease at all, so check that before planning a boiler up there. Delivery to the upper flat means the shared front door and stairs, so the neighbour below should know the date. Where the two flats are replacing boilers around the same time, one visit to look at both positions can save each owner a problem later. Balham’s streets are largely permit parking, so a visitor permit helps. Your Gas Safe installer notifies building control, and a certificate reaches you afterwards.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Our boiler is in the loft of our upper flat. Can the new one go there?",
      "a": "Yes, if the loft is set up for it. That means a fixed ladder, a permanent light, a boarded path to the boiler and a guard around the hatch, and many older loft installs have none of these. The condensate pipe also needs protecting from frost. If fitting all that is awkward, moving the boiler down to a cupboard is worth pricing alongside."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|dulwich": {
    "local": "Dulwich houses are large and mostly still single homes, so the question is often less about the boiler than about the hot water. A family with several bathrooms usually needs a well-sized cylinder, and where it sits decides how long the top-floor taps take to run hot. Victorian houses here sometimes keep the boiler in the cellar. There is space down there, but condensate may need pumping up to a drain, and the flue has to reach outside at a low level, clear of the front steps and paths. Roads are broad and many houses have drives, though school-run traffic around the village is heavy at the start and end of the day, so deliveries go better mid-morning. External work may need the Estate’s consent, but the building regulations side is simpler: the Gas Safe engineer notifies building control once the job is done.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Is weather compensation worth having in a house this size?",
      "a": "Often, yes. Weather compensation uses an outside sensor to lower the boiler’s flow temperature on mild days and raise it on cold ones. The boiler runs cooler for more of the year, which lets it condense and work more efficiently, and the house warms more evenly. It suits large houses with plenty of radiators well. It is simple to add at installation and harder to retrofit, so decide before the boiler is chosen."
    }
  },
  "boiler-installation|acton": {
    "local": "Acton’s older streets are full of converted flats and shared houses, where one boiler now serves far more people than the original household. That shifts the decision towards hot water capacity rather than heating output. Flats above shops on the High Street share entrances and back yards with the businesses below, so deliveries and the old boiler’s removal are timed around them. Many terraces had the kitchen extended and the boiler moved into the extension, sometimes with a flue that now sits under a neighbour’s window or a new rooflight. A new installation is the moment to put that right. In a shared house the landlord arranges the work, and the tenants should hear the date well ahead. Parking in W3 is largely permit-controlled near the town centre. The Gas Safe engineer notifies building control, so the paperwork is in order when you come to sell or remortgage.",
    "faq2": {
      "q": "Do we need a water softener with a new combi?",
      "a": "Not necessarily, but you do need scale protection. West London water is hard, and limescale builds up in a combi’s hot water side, cutting performance and shortening its life. Current guidance calls for scale protection on a new combi in a hard water area, usually an inline scale reducer fitted on the cold supply. A whole-house softener is optional and separate, and if you fit one, keep an unsoftened tap for drinking water."
    }
  }
};
